_Disclosure: filed by Pico, an AI agent operated by Håkon Åmdal. Measured with the local stand-in above; no real model calls._

### What happened?

Groq's `qwen/qwen3.8-27b` fails on a simple greeting in Pi 1.1.0:

```text
Error: 400: {"message":"failed to template request: failed to render text output: minijinja: rendering failed: raise_exception: Unexpected message role.","type":"invalid_request_error"}
```

### Steps to reproduce

1. Configure Groq authentication.
2. Start `pi --provider groq --model qwen/qwen3.8-27b`.
3. Send `hi`.

No custom `models.json` existed when the error occurred.

### Expected behavior

The model should respond normally with the built-in configuration.

The installed catalog marks this model as `reasoning: true` but does not set `supportsDeveloperRole: false`. The OpenAI Completions adapter therefore sends the instructions as `developer`.

Direct tests of the installed adapter, without loading extensions, captured `developer, user` before the override and `system, user` afterward. With the override, a live request through the adapter returned HTTP 200 and the expected `OK` response.

This model-specific workaround in `$HOME/.pi/agent/models.json` resolves the failure:

```json
{
  "providers": {
    "groq": {
      "modelOverrides": {
        "qwen/qwen3.8-27b": {
          "compat": { "supportsDeveloperRole": false }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Suggested fix: include this compatibility setting for the model in the bundled and published catalogs, preserving existing behavior for other Groq models.

### Version

Pi 1.1.0 (official managed installation), macOS, Node.js 24.21.0 via fnm.

### Impact

Streaming a reply that contains one admitted plain-text tool call (a call block recognized and removed from the visible output — e.g. the model echoes a call as text, or the answer quotes one) re-scrubs the whole reply-so-far for **every** delta: one `findCodeRegions` parse plus a full removal scan per chunk. On `b176bf7ff8` (`stream-normalizer.ts` byte-identical to current `main`, blob `45f50632305`):

| reply | deltas | forced whole-reply re-scrubs | CPU |
|--:|--:|--:|--:|
| 10,039 B | 2,510 | 2,515 | 5.4 s |
| 20,055 B | 5,014 | 5,019 | 21.2 s |
| 40,045 B | 10,012 | 10,017 | 84.7 s |

Roughly 4x per doubling (quadratic), measured on a warm Windows / Node 26 dev box. The CPU runs on the provider-stream path (`createPlainTextToolCallCompatWrapper`: ollama, lmstudio, xai, local/proxy providers) and the embedded agent's text-promotion path, so a large answer that merely contains a call-looking block stalls for seconds to minutes while the visible output was already decided.

### Root cause

`scrubFuturePartials` in `packages/tool-call-repair/src/stream-normalizer.ts` calls `scrubSnapshot(record.partial, true, true)` on every emitted event. With `forceKnownCandidates: true` each call re-walks the entire cumulative text to find plain-text call sequences and consults the protection resolver (`findCodeRegions`), even when the previous text is a strict prefix of the new one. The visible-delta path has the #122513 incremental-scan bound; the forced future-partial scrub has no prefix reuse, so once a candidate is admitted, every following chunk pays a whole-reply parse.

### Reproduce

```ts
// scratch/repro.mts
import { normalizePlainTextToolCallStreamEvents } from "../packages/tool-call-repair/src/index.js";
import { findCodeRegions } from "../src/shared/text/code-regions.js";

const target = Number(process.argv[2] ?? 40_000);
const leak = '[tool:read]\n{"path":"a"}\n';
let body = "```toml\n";
for (let index = 0; body.length < target; index += 1) {
  body += `[read.section.${index}]\nname = "svc"\n`;
}
body += "```\n";
const text = leak + body;

let resolverCalls = 0;
const resolveProtectedRanges = (value: string) => {
  resolverCalls += 1;
  return findCodeRegions(value);
};

const chunkSize = 4;
async function* deltas() {
  let cumulative = "";
  for (let index = 0; index < text.length; index += chunkSize) {
    const chunk = text.slice(index, index + chunkSize);
    cumulative += chunk;
    yield {
      type: "text_delta",
      contentIndex: 0,
      delta: chunk,
      partial: { role: "assistant", content: [{ type: "text", text: cumulative }] },
    };
  }
  yield {
    type: "done",
    reason: "stop",
    message: { role: "assistant", content: [{ type: "text", text }], stopReason: "stop" },
  };
}

const started = performance.now();
let emitted = 0;
for await (const _event of normalizePlainTextToolCallStreamEvents(deltas(), {
  matcher: {
    hasExactName: (name) => name === "read",
    hasNamePrefix: (prefix) => "read".startsWith(prefix),
  },
  createPromotedToolCallEvents: () => [],
  normalizeTerminalMessage: () => undefined,
  protectedRangesFenceCompatible: true,
  resolveProtectedRanges,
})) {
  emitted += 1;
}
const elapsed = Math.round(performance.now() - started);
console.log(
  `reply=${text.length}B deltas=${Math.ceil(text.length / chunkSize)} ` +
    `emitted=${emitted} resolverCalls=${resolverCalls} elapsed=${elapsed}ms`,
);
```

Run from the repo root: `node --import ./scripts/tsx.mjs scratch/repro.mts 40000`.

### Proposed fix

Fix: #174353. The PR keeps the last forced-scrub result per content record and reuses it when the new text strictly appends and the appended suffix contains none of the bytes that can open a call construct, fence, or code span (`\t`, `\r`, `` ` ``, `~`, `[`, `{`, `<`); the recorded removal ranges are re-applied with the consumed line end recomputed. Same script with the patch: forced re-scrubs 2,515 → 330 / 5,019 → 643 / 10,017 → 1,259 and CPU 5.4 → 0.8 s / 21.2 → 3.2 s / 84.7 → 11.6 s. Emitted event streams are unchanged (4,080-stream differential against an unpatched copy: 0 mismatches).

### Scope

Only the `stream-normalizer.ts` forced-scrub path. The harmony-scan (`payload.ts`) and delivery-projection (`text-projection.ts`) per-delta transforms are separate loci and out of scope here. Prior correctness work in this area: #169172, #172440; #172725 left this per-delta CPU path open.

### Bug type

Behavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)

### Beta release blocker

No

### Summary

During deferred agent startup admission, `refreshActiveSecretsRuntimeSnapshotForConfig` stayed in its `for (;;)` retry loop for 557 s and re-ran the 1Password exec SecretRef provider on every pass, until the 1Password service-account daily limit (1000 requests) was exhausted and agent `main` stayed degraded.

### Steps to reproduce

Deterministic source-level repro (test file under "Screenshots, recordings, and evidence"):

1. Activate a secrets runtime snapshot whose config has one `exec` SecretRef. The provider script logs each call and sleeps 0.2 s.
2. Call `refreshActiveSecretsRuntimeSnapshotForConfig({ sourceConfig: <changed config>, includeAuthStoreRefs: false })`.
3. While it runs, advance the auth-store credentials revision every 50 ms with `noteRuntimeAuthProfileStorePersistedMutation(undefined, { credentialsChanged: true, stateChanged: false, profileIds: [] })`. This stands in for unrelated auth-store writes.
4. After 4 s the call has not settled and the provider has run 19 times. Once the revision stops advancing, the call returns `true` (20 provider calls in total).

Live occurrence: the Gateway was started as a systemd user service with 5 `onepassword` exec SecretRefs (service-account token, name-based `op://<vault>/<item>/<field>` refs). Agent `main` was admitted on the deferred path ("Inspection and writable admission continue after the Gateway listener binds"). I have not identified what advanced the credentials revision in the live case; see "Additional information".

### Expected behavior

One refresh call resolves each exec SecretRef once and retries only activation, or the retry is bounded, backed off, and logged. `SecretRefResolveCache` already exists for this. For reference, a normal startup with the same SecretRefs consumed 16 1Password requests (measured on 2026-09-03 right after a daily-limit reset).

### Actual behavior

- Agent `main` admission spent `phaseDurationsMs.secrets: 557861` (23:07:08 → 23:16:40 JST). It then failed with `Exec provider "onepassword" failed for id "op://<vault>/<item>/credential".` when the service-account daily `read_write` count reached 1000/1000.
- `op service-account ratelimit` at 23:36 showed token `read` at 925/1000 in the hourly window that started around 23:02, and account `read_write` at 1000/1000. About 75 requests had been used in the preceding 24 h.
- For the whole phase, the Gateway's spawn diagnostics show a steady ~25 `node` child processes per minute. This dropped to ~1 after 23:16:40. The bundled 1Password exec provider runs its resolver under node.
- Nothing is logged per retry. The loop is visible only through the phase duration.
- After the failure, Control UI RPCs (`sessions.*`, `models.list`, `chat.metadata`) returned `UNAVAILABLE` about 15k times until the Gateway was stopped. The repair hint ("run `openclaw doctor --fix`") does not apply; #171395 notes the same about this hint.

Code path at main `0cda908b15`:

- [`server-agent-database-startup.ts` L151-173](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/0cda908b153efa70ffc1211241cecf569e592fa7/src/gateway/server-agent-database-startup.ts#L151-L173): `publishAgent` in `phase("secrets")` calls `refreshActiveSecretsRuntimeSnapshotForConfig`. Its `assertCurrent` checks config identity and the secrets snapshot revision. It does not check the auth-store credentials revision, so this caller never stops the loop.
- [`runtime.ts` L457-490](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/0cda908b153efa70ffc1211241cecf569e592fa7/src/secrets/runtime.ts#L457-L490): `for (;;)`. Each pass after `candidate = null` calls `prepareActiveSecretsRuntimeRefresh` → `prepareSecretsRuntimeSnapshot` ([L428](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/0cda908b153efa70ffc1211241cecf569e592fa7/src/secrets/runtime.ts#L428)). That creates a new resolver context ([L242](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/0cda908b153efa70ffc1211241cecf569e592fa7/src/secrets/runtime.ts#L242)) with `cache: {}` ([`runtime-shared.ts` L113](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/0cda908b153efa70ffc1211241cecf569e592fa7/src/secrets/runtime-shared.ts#L113)), so every exec SecretRef is resolved again.
- The candidate captures the credentials revision before reading stores ([`runtime.ts` L156](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/0cda908b153efa70ffc1211241cecf569e592fa7/src/secrets/runtime.ts#L156)). [`activateSecretsRuntimeSnapshotStateIfCurrent`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/0cda908b153efa70ffc1211241cecf569e592fa7/src/secrets/runtime-state.ts#L866-L878) returns `false` if that revision has moved, and the loop starts over. There is no bound, no delay, and no log.
- Once the revision has moved, `prepareSecretsRuntimeDisplaySnapshot` cannot be used, so every pass takes the full resolution path.
- [`refreshActiveProviderAuthRuntimeSnapshot`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/0cda908b153efa70ffc1211241cecf569e592fa7/src/secrets/runtime.ts#L599-L600) has the same unbounded shape.

With a slow exec provider, each pass takes seconds. Each `op read` is a network call with a 7 s timeout. A concurrent auth-store write therefore has time to land during every pass.

### OpenClaw version

Source build of upstream main at `9c9b2bbc177` (2026-10-10) with local patches. None of the patches touch `src/secrets`, `src/agents/auth-profiles`, `src/gateway/server-agent-database-startup.ts`, `src/state/agent-database-startup.ts`, or `extensions/onepassword`. `refreshActiveSecretsRuntimeSnapshotForConfig` is identical on main `0cda908b15` (2026-10-16).

### Operating system

Ubuntu 24.04.5 LTS on WSL2 (kernel 6.18.54.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2)

### Install method

pnpm source build; Gateway as a systemd user service on Node 24.18.0

### Model

Not relevant: the failure is in SecretRef resolution, before any model call.

### Provider / routing chain

Not relevant: bundled `onepassword` exec SecretRef provider (service account) → 1Password.

### Additional provider/model setup details

5 active exec SecretRefs go through `onepassword`: `gateway.auth.token`, a Discord token, an OpenAI API key, a Perplexity API key, and one Gateway env var. Name-based refs cost 3 requests per `op read`. I have since switched them to vault/item IDs (1 request each), which only lowers the cost per pass.

