Not tested: model execution itself is not tested (no CLI binary or provider on the test host); the run legs show only which boundary each run reached (provider layer, account/route refusal, CLI-runner account check), and an unbound CLI job's run outcome is unchanged.

## Merge Danger

Non-CLI references are unchanged: an embedded-runtime `@<profileId>` still resolves its account and still refuses when it is missing. Only `cron.update` is touched; creation, delivery, and run paths are untouched. A bound job that moves to a CLI-backed model keeps the actionable rejection rather than being accepted and left with an account its next run refuses; clearing that account needs a store-level signal that does not exist today and belongs in its own change. An accepted unbound edit still needs no pinned profile, or a resolvable account, to run.

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes Windows Scheduled Task gateway stops that report a closed LAN port as unverified when its TCP refusal arrives after the fixed 250 ms probe timeout.

Fixes #174563

## Why This Change Was Made

Port confirmation now shares the existing five-second settlement window across required host probes. An inconclusive probe stays distinct from a confirmed busy listener.

## User Impact

A delayed `ECONNREFUSED` can confirm a released port before the stop-settlement deadline, while a live listener remains busy.

## Evidence

- `./node_modules/.bin/vitest run src/infra/ports-probe.test.ts src/daemon/schtasks.stop.test.ts -t 'uses the remaining probe budget|keeps inconclusive gateway port settlement distinct'` — 2 passed.
- `./node_modules/.bin/tsx proof-live.mjs .` imports the production inspection path and uses a real loopback bind with a controlled delayed refusal at `net.connect`. The delay is controlled at the connection boundary; this does not claim a run on the affected Windows host.

```text
