## What Problem This Solves

Windows `gateway stop` and restart can report a closed LAN port as busy when connection refusal takes longer than the fixed 250 ms probe. This also interrupts the managed-service shutdown step used by updates.

Fixes #174563.

## User Impact

A delayed refusal can now positively confirm shutdown within the existing five-second settlement window. An accepted connection still means busy; an exhausted or failed probe reports inconclusive settlement and points to `openclaw gateway status --deep`. Timeout is never treated as proof that the port is free.

## Why This Change Was Made

Carry one absolute settlement deadline through native inspection and TCP confirmation, with cancellation for inspection work. Keep the normal 250 ms default for callers without a settlement deadline. Positive TCP confirmation remains necessary because a successful scoped bind can coexist with a wildcard listener on macOS. Move Windows port waiting into the existing port-diagnostics owner and migrate both stop/restart callers, removing the old process-module export instead of adding a lint suppression.

Supersedes #174598, thanks @sunlit-deng. No config, CLI, dependency, or database schema changes.

## Evidence

- Baseline `b921f805a3e535642d29c232c22ece7c831cb5a1`: a simulated two-second `ECONNREFUSED` returned `false`; an expired probe also returned `false` instead of an inconclusive diagnostic.
- `OPENCLAW_VITEST_RUNTIME=node node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/daemon/schtasks-port-release.test.ts --maxWorkers=1 --testTimeout=5000`: all 3 cases pass (delayed refusal, accepted connection, refusal beyond the deadline). Test time was 181 ms; total wall time was 182 s, dominated by shared compiler/setup work.
- Targeted tsgo (including the repository ambient declarations), oxlint, oxfmt, `git diff --check`, and madge: passed; 0 import cycles.
- Real macOS sockets: native inspection observed busy, then confirmed free 133 ms after close. A fresh production Gateway started through the source server API with isolated HOME/state and a free loopback port; lifecycle teardown was positively confirmed free in 137 ms. Cold source startup took about 121 s. The ordinary CLI bootstrap refused runtime-artifact publication when it could not establish offline service state, so no installed Gateway was changed. Native Windows timing is simulated, not claimed as a Windows live run.
- Independent Codex review: no actionable P0–P2 findings.
- Importing suites plus stop/settlement siblings: `OPENCLAW_VITEST_RUNTIME=node OPENCLAW_NODE_TEST_PLAN_CONTINUE_ON_FAILURE=1 OPENCLAW_E2E_USE_PREBUILT_DIST=1 node scripts/run-vitest.mjs <55 selected files> --maxWorkers=1` passed all 8 shards in 875.62 s: 54 files passed, 1 skipped; 1,018 tests passed, 14 skipped. This includes real macOS launchd lifecycle coverage (9 tests, 53.3 s); native Windows integration remains platform-skipped on macOS.
- Existing slow updater fixtures: `update-cli.windows-lifecycle.test.ts` spent 234.4 s across 27 cases; `update-cli.service-authority.test.ts` spent 178.5 s across 18 cases. These repeatedly run full update/finalization fixtures; the changed stop waiter is outside their mocked native lifecycle. No timeout/retry/assertion changes.
- Full `check:changed`, including its SQLite inventory, was omitted under the requested local validation budget; the focused checks above replace that local run and PR CI remains the broad authority.

Production: +29 net lines; tests: +85; docs: +6. The additional production lines carry/cancel the shared deadline and distinguish uncertainty; the process module shrinks by 15 lines.

