Affected versions: confirmed against the current default branch as of 2026-10-12

## Summary

LobsterAI's "Export Providers" feature encrypts each configured provider's API key with AES-GCM using a key derived (PBKDF2, 100000 iterations) from a password, intended to protect the exported file's contents. The default export path, the one reached by the "Export Providers" button with no prompt for a custom password, always uses a password that is a literal constant in the open-source repository (`EXPORT_PASSWORD = 'lobsterai-APP'` in `src/renderer/constants/app.ts`). The same constant is used on import when the user supplies no custom password. Because this value is public and identical across every installation, anyone who obtains an export file produced by the default flow can decrypt every API key it contains with no other information.

## Details

`src/renderer/constants/app.ts`:
```typescript
export const EXPORT_PASSWORD = 'lobsterai-APP';
```

`src/renderer/components/Settings.tsx`:
```typescript
const DEFAULT_EXPORT_PASSWORD = EXPORT_PASSWORD;

const handleExportProviders = async () => {
  const payload = await buildProvidersExport(DEFAULT_EXPORT_PASSWORD);
};
```
```typescript
apiKey = await decryptWithPassword(apiKeyObj, DEFAULT_EXPORT_PASSWORD);
```

`handleExportProviders` takes no password input from the user; it always calls `buildProvidersExport` with the hardcoded constant. The import path's default decryption attempt uses the identical constant. `src/renderer/services/encryption.ts` implements the AES-GCM/PBKDF2 cryptography; the salt is randomized per export, but since the password itself is public and constant, this provides no real protection, anyone can derive the same key from the salt embedded in the file plus the public password.

## Steps To Reproduce

(available upon request)

## Impact

Anyone who obtains a LobsterAI providers-export file produced through the default "Export Providers" action, whether through a shared backup, a synced cloud drive, an email or support attachment, or accidental public exposure, can recover every provider API key it contains with no other information, using only the publicly available source code. This directly compromises third-party AI provider credentials, enabling unauthorized API usage or billing abuse.

